### Instruction Set Architecture (ISA)

I know a lot of resources like to explain the history of CPUs, which explains why we now have different models of CPU. I won't dig into that subject, so as to be more straightforward and get to the point.
An ISA is defined as follows: An ISA is the boundary between hardware and software. It defines the vocabulary of the CPU: which instructions exist, which registers are available, and how data is handled.
So, we have different kinds of CPU. Here are the three biggest kinds you'll hear about:
•	x86 (what we are going to use primarily in this series of articles)
•	ARM
•	RISC-V

Obviously there are many more CPUs in the embedded programming world or in HPC (High-Performance Computing) area.
x86 is from the CISC family (Complex Instruction Set Computer), while ARM and RISC-V are from the RISC family (Reduced Instruction Set Computer).

None of this changes the basics we saw earlier about cache, RAM, disk, etc. But the instructions in assembly are different! The number and names of registers, or the operations, are not called the same way.

### Is one better than the other ? 

Well, the short answer is "No". The longer answer is "Yes, probably, depending on the situation".
ARM is mostly used in the phone industry. x86 is mostly used in computers like the one you are using. RISC-V is an open-source ISA used in various areas.

The main argument behind them is the trade-off between computing power and energy consumption. At the end of the day, as we said when introducing computers, they only use electricity to flip bits.

Over time we've seen that the idea of "reduced instructions" from the RISC family is losing its grip, while x86 keeps developing and improving its performance a lot. So we don't know yet how things are going to evolve.

