### Syntax

For some reasons, once again history matters but we will not go too deep into it, even on x86 you can write your assembly two different ways ! 
 
The second one is the Intel syntax, while the first one is the AT&T syntax. Some old books and some universities curriculum still use AT&T, but we won’t see that. We will use the Intel syntax.

One of the big differences is the way we write a simple assignment.

We want to put the value 0x42 inside a register; 

Intel ; mov rdi, 0x42
AT&T ; movq $0x42, %rdi

So while Intel is doing something more like 
rdi = 0x42 

very close to what we do as programmers usually (destination first, source second).

With the AT&T syntax you are doing ; 
0x42 = rdi 

Which puts the source first and the destination in second place.

We are going to stick to x86 but you have at least the knowledge sufficient to understand there is way more thing outside our little experiment. 
